theory.intro:
The $x$-intercept is where a line crosses the $x$-axis, and the $y$-intercept is where it crosses the $y$-axis. Read them off a graph, or identify them from a table.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-1.5,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.3) -- (0,4.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->] (-0.3,3.36) -- (3.2,-0.84);
\fill (0,3) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (2.5,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right,xshift=4pt] at (0,3) {$(0,\,b)$};
\node[below left,xshift=-4pt] at (2.5,0) {$(a,\,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
